---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";

const metrics = [
  { value: "$557.35M", label: "Shooter genre sales, 4th of all genres" },
  { value: "$158.76M", label: "Activision shooter sales, top of four" },
  { value: "42.74%", label: "Activision portfolio in Shooter" },
  { value: "53.09%", label: "X360 share of Activision's NA shooter sales" },
];

const links = [
  { label: "Deck (PDF)", href: "/media/Linh_Le_Publisher_Selection_Analysis.pdf" },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="The strongest financials belonged to the publisher we did not choose"
  description="Selecting a publishing partner for a shooter title on Xbox 360, where the commercial screen and the financial screen disagreed and the recommendation had to say why. Case study by Linh (Min) Le."
  project="Video Game Publisher Selection"
  period="February to March 2026"
  tint="finance"
  hook="Ubisoft came out ahead on every growth measure we tested. We recommended Activision anyway, and the interesting part of this analysis is the argument for why."
  metrics={metrics}
  links={links}
>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">The decision this informed</h2>
        <p>
          A studio with a first-person shooter built for Xbox 360 has to choose a publishing partner,
          and the choice commits marketing budget, distribution reach and a revenue share for the life
          of the product. Four candidates were on the table: Nintendo, Sony Computer Entertainment,
          Activision and Ubisoft.
        </p>
        <p>
          The brief asked for a recommendation defended on evidence. I worked as co-analyst across the
          market sizing and the financial statement analysis, on a team of four.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">The finding</h2>
        <p>
          The commercial case for Activision is unambiguous. The shooter genre accounts for
          <strong>$557.35 million</strong> in historical sales, ranking fourth of all genres at
          <strong>11.74%</strong> of the market. Activision leads the four candidates with
          <strong>$158.76 million</strong> of that, holds the largest regional share almost everywhere
          including North America where shooter sales are strongest, and commits
          <strong>42.74% of its own sales portfolio</strong> to the genre. The other three publishers
          are diversified, so shooters are a line of business for them and a centre of gravity for
          Activision.
        </p>
        <Figure
          src="/figures/publisher-genre-sales.png"
          alt="Bar chart of total sales by video game genre with Shooter highlighted at 557.35 million dollars, alongside a table giving each genre's percentage of total sales."
          caption="Total sales across twelve genres. Shooter ranks fourth at $557.35M and 11.74% of the market, which is what established the genre was worth entering before any publisher question was asked."
          wide
        />
        <p>
          The platform screen points the same way. Xbox 360 is the leading console for shooter sales in
          North America at <strong>$125.54 million</strong>, Activision generates more of that than any
          other publisher, and <strong>53.09%</strong> of Activision's North American shooter sales come
          from that one platform. Part of the reason is structural: Activision and Ubisoft are the only
          third-party publishers of the four competing on Xbox 360 at all, since Nintendo and Sony
          publish primarily onto their own hardware.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then the financial screen disagreed. Across the comparison window Activision showed positive
          growth in revenue, operating income, profit and assets, and its return on equity improved
          through profit margin and asset turnover while financial leverage came down, which is returns
          earned from operations instead of borrowed from the balance sheet. Activision outperformed all
          three rivals on asset turnover by a wide margin.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ubisoft still came out ahead across the growth measures. On the financial screen alone, the
          recommendation would have been Ubisoft.
        </p>
        <p>
          We recommended Activision, and the reason is the whole point of the analysis. Financial
          strength qualifies a partner and does not by itself sell a shooter. Activision's advantage is
          genre-specific and platform-specific, built on a catalogue already concentrated where this
          title would launch, and that specialisation is not something a stronger balance sheet
          substitutes for.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">What I did, and the obvious alternative I rejected</h2>
        <p>
          The obvious alternative was to rank the four on shooter sales and stop, which would have
          produced the same name by a route that collapses under a single follow-up question. Sales
          leadership establishes that a publisher has sold games in a genre. It says nothing about
          whether the company can fund a launch, sustain distribution, or survive the window between
          signature and release.
        </p>
        <div class="pull">
          Return on equity has the same weakness in miniature. Two publishers can post identical
          returns while one earns them through margin and asset efficiency and the other manufactures
          them with debt. Decomposing the ratio into its three components is the only way to tell those
          apart from outside the company, and it is what showed Activision improving on the operating
          side while reducing leverage.
        </div>
        <p>
          So the analysis ran the commercial case and the financial case as genuinely separate tests,
          which is what made the disagreement visible. A single blended score would have averaged
          Ubisoft's financial strength against Activision's genre concentration and produced a ranking
          that hid the actual decision. Keeping them apart forced the trade-off into the open and
          required the recommendation to argue for it rather than assert it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Reading the comparison window across all four publishers rather than Activision alone is what
          allowed shared industry pressure to be identified as an industry condition instead of a
          company-specific warning sign. Every publisher in the set showed strong DuPont growth, which
          is the context that makes Activision's asset turnover advantage meaningful rather than merely
          positive.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <header class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Evidence</p>
        <h2 class="h-sub">The deck as presented</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="embed">
        <iframe src="/media/Linh_Le_Publisher_Selection_Analysis.pdf"
                title="Video game publisher selection deck" loading="lazy"></iframe>
      </div>
      <p class="embed-note">
        <a href="/media/Linh_Le_Publisher_Selection_Analysis.pdf">Open the deck in its own tab</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">Assumptions and limitations</h2>
        <p>
          The comparison set is four publishers. That is enough to rank them against one another and too
          few to say anything about the industry, so every relative statement here is bounded by that
          set.
        </p>
        <p>
          The financial analysis covers a two-year comparison window drawn from public filings. Two
          years establishes direction and cannot establish durability. A commitment of this size would
          ordinarily want a longer series alongside forward guidance that public statements do not
          disclose.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sales data is historical and tied to a console generation. The Xbox 360 findings describe
          where the audience was in the data rather than where it would be at launch, so the platform
          result is evidence about publisher specialisation instead of a forecast.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nothing here models deal terms, and they are what ultimately decide whether a good partner
          produces a good outcome. Revenue share, marketing commitment and creative control sit inside
          a negotiation that no external analysis can see.
        </p>
        <p>
          The revenue picture is also incomplete by construction. The dataset covers unit sales and
          carries nothing on in-game purchases, which for a modern shooter is a substantial and growing
          share of what a title actually earns.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</CaseStudy>
